2026-06-05 17:19:59.203 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x1654c3800>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x1320a3b60>>, "[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING CURRICULUM- Zamora.docx - Introduction]\nSIX-MONTH TRAINING CURRICULUM FOR SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE PRACTICES (ALIGNED WITH TVET AND UVITAB STANDARDS)\nLEAD PROJECT (COMMUNITY LEARNING GARDENS)\nBackground\nThe LEAD (Local, Empowerment and Development) Project is being implemented in the Palabek Refugee Settlement in Lamwo District, Uganda. Its primary goal is to combat the pervasive issues of chronic food insecurity and poverty affecting both refugees and the host communities.\nThe project addresses challenges like limited land availability and unpredictable rainfall by promoting climate-smart agriculture (CSA). The core of the agricultural training is conducted at community learning gardens. One garden is located on land allocated by the Office of the Prime Minister for the refugee community, and the other is on land provided by the host community. These gardens serve as practical learning hubs where participants gain hands-on skills that they can then apply to their own household gardens.\nTo ensure the long-term sustainability and economic empowerment of participants, the agricultural training is integrated with financial literacy education and the establishment of Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLA). This component aims to improve participants' ability to save money, access credit, and engage effectively with local markets.\nKey Features of the Program:\nThe LEAD Project's key features are designed to provide a comprehensive, practical, and inclusive pathway to self-reliance for refugees and host communities. Below are summary of the main points:\nPractical and Timely Training: The program delivers hands-on agricultural training that is carefully aligned with the local seasonal cycles. This ensures that participants learn and apply skills at the most relevant times, from land preparation and planting to harvesting.\nStandards-Based Curriculum: The training is based on the Competency-Based Education and Training (CBET) approach, following the standards of Uganda's Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) system and the Uganda Vocational Qualifications Framework (UVQF). This means the skills taught are officially recognized and directly relevant to the job market. The curriculum is modular, allowing participants to acquire recognized qualifications in agribusiness.\nInclusivity: The project is specifically designed to be accessible to a wide range of participants, including women, men, youth, and persons with disabilities and individuals with low literacy levels. This ensures that the benefits of the program reach a diverse and often marginalized audience.\nIntegrated Approach: The training goes beyond just agriculture by incorporating crucial cross-cutting themes. These include nutrition, gender equity, protection, Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene (WASH), and environmental sustainability which are all vital for improving overall household well-being and resilience.\nFuture Pathways: The program is not a dead end; it provides clear opportunities for participants to advance. They can either transition to more advanced vocational training or use the skills and financial literacy they have gained to establish their own self-reliant farming enterprises.\nOverall Goal\nThe overall goal of the LEAD Project is to empower refugees and host communities in Palabek by providing them with practical agricultural and financial skills. The project aims to achieve four main outcomes:\nImprove Food and Nutrition Security: Equip households with the skills to produce their own food, thereby ensuring they have enough to eat and improving their nutritional status.\nEnhance Resilience to Climate Shocks: Build the community's capacity to withstand environmental challenges like droughts and unpredictable rainfall through the adoption of climate-smart agriculture.\nPromote Economic Empowerment: Enable participants to transition from subsistence farming to market-oriented production and savings by linking them with Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) and formal financial institutions.\nStrengthen Community Cohesion: Foster peaceful coexistence and collaboration between refugees and host communities by encouraging joint agricultural and business ventures.\nProject Objectives\nThe LEAD Project has several specific objectives aimed at equipping refugees and host communities with practical skills for a more secure and sustainable future. These objectives are:\nPromote Climate-Smart Farming: The project will help participants adopt climate-smart agriculture (CSA) techniques, with a focus on drought-tolerant crops such as sorghum, millet, maize, cassava, and tobacco.\nImprove Soil and Crop Health: It will promote integrated soil fertility and pest management, using a combination of both organic and artificial methods to ensure healthy crops and sustainable practices.\nScale up Household Gardens: The project will support participants in replicating the skills learned at the community gardens to their own homes, establishing household gardens that provide a continuous supply of food for both personal consumption and commercial sale.\nEnhance Financial Skills: The project will introduce financial literacy and the Village Savings and Loan Association (VSLA) methodology to help participants with saving, accessing loans, and engaging in collective marketing efforts.\nEncourage Community Learning: The project aims to foster a culture of peer learning, gender inclusivity, and knowledge sharing within the community. This also serves the purpose of promoting peaceful coexistence between the refugee and host communities.\nTarget Participants\nThe program is designed for a diverse group of individuals from both the refugee and host communities. The project aims to be inclusive, welcoming men, women, youth, and people with disabilities (PWDs).\nParticipants are expected to have some level of subsistence or small-scale farming experience, regardless of their literacy level. A key requirement is that they have access to their own household plots or land where they can apply the skills learned at the community gardens.\nThe project specifically seeks out individuals who are committed to joining or forming Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) to help them develop agricultural enterprises.", False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 17:19:59.205 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #8 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x1654c3800>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x1320a3b60>>, "[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING CURRICULUM- Zamora.docx - Introduction]\nSIX-MONTH TRAINING CURRICULUM FOR SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE PRACTICES (ALIGNED WITH TVET AND UVITAB STANDARDS)\nLEAD PROJECT (COMMUNITY LEARNING GARDENS)\nBackground\nThe LEAD (Local, Empowerment and Development) Project is being implemented in the Palabek Refugee Settlement in Lamwo District, Uganda. Its primary goal is to combat the pervasive issues of chronic food insecurity and poverty affecting both refugees and the host communities.\nThe project addresses challenges like limited land availability and unpredictable rainfall by promoting climate-smart agriculture (CSA). The core of the agricultural training is conducted at community learning gardens. One garden is located on land allocated by the Office of the Prime Minister for the refugee community, and the other is on land provided by the host community. These gardens serve as practical learning hubs where participants gain hands-on skills that they can then apply to their own household gardens.\nTo ensure the long-term sustainability and economic empowerment of participants, the agricultural training is integrated with financial literacy education and the establishment of Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLA). This component aims to improve participants' ability to save money, access credit, and engage effectively with local markets.\nKey Features of the Program:\nThe LEAD Project's key features are designed to provide a comprehensive, practical, and inclusive pathway to self-reliance for refugees and host communities. Below are summary of the main points:\nPractical and Timely Training: The program delivers hands-on agricultural training that is carefully aligned with the local seasonal cycles. This ensures that participants learn and apply skills at the most relevant times, from land preparation and planting to harvesting.\nStandards-Based Curriculum: The training is based on the Competency-Based Education and Training (CBET) approach, following the standards of Uganda's Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) system and the Uganda Vocational Qualifications Framework (UVQF). This means the skills taught are officially recognized and directly relevant to the job market. The curriculum is modular, allowing participants to acquire recognized qualifications in agribusiness.\nInclusivity: The project is specifically designed to be accessible to a wide range of participants, including women, men, youth, and persons with disabilities and individuals with low literacy levels. This ensures that the benefits of the program reach a diverse and often marginalized audience.\nIntegrated Approach: The training goes beyond just agriculture by incorporating crucial cross-cutting themes. These include nutrition, gender equity, protection, Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene (WASH), and environmental sustainability which are all vital for improving overall household well-being and resilience.\nFuture Pathways: The program is not a dead end; it provides clear opportunities for participants to advance. They can either transition to more advanced vocational training or use the skills and financial literacy they have gained to establish their own self-reliant farming enterprises.\nOverall Goal\nThe overall goal of the LEAD Project is to empower refugees and host communities in Palabek by providing them with practical agricultural and financial skills. The project aims to achieve four main outcomes:\nImprove Food and Nutrition Security: Equip households with the skills to produce their own food, thereby ensuring they have enough to eat and improving their nutritional status.\nEnhance Resilience to Climate Shocks: Build the community's capacity to withstand environmental challenges like droughts and unpredictable rainfall through the adoption of climate-smart agriculture.\nPromote Economic Empowerment: Enable participants to transition from subsistence farming to market-oriented production and savings by linking them with Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) and formal financial institutions.\nStrengthen Community Cohesion: Foster peaceful coexistence and collaboration between refugees and host communities by encouraging joint agricultural and business ventures.\nProject Objectives\nThe LEAD Project has several specific objectives aimed at equipping refugees and host communities with practical skills for a more secure and sustainable future. These objectives are:\nPromote Climate-Smart Farming: The project will help participants adopt climate-smart agriculture (CSA) techniques, with a focus on drought-tolerant crops such as sorghum, millet, maize, cassava, and tobacco.\nImprove Soil and Crop Health: It will promote integrated soil fertility and pest management, using a combination of both organic and artificial methods to ensure healthy crops and sustainable practices.\nScale up Household Gardens: The project will support participants in replicating the skills learned at the community gardens to their own homes, establishing household gardens that provide a continuous supply of food for both personal consumption and commercial sale.\nEnhance Financial Skills: The project will introduce financial literacy and the Village Savings and Loan Association (VSLA) methodology to help participants with saving, accessing loans, and engaging in collective marketing efforts.\nEncourage Community Learning: The project aims to foster a culture of peer learning, gender inclusivity, and knowledge sharing within the community. This also serves the purpose of promoting peaceful coexistence between the refugee and host communities.\nTarget Participants\nThe program is designed for a diverse group of individuals from both the refugee and host communities. The project aims to be inclusive, welcoming men, women, youth, and people with disabilities (PWDs).\nParticipants are expected to have some level of subsistence or small-scale farming experience, regardless of their literacy level. A key requirement is that they have access to their own household plots or land where they can apply the skills learned at the community gardens.\nThe project specifically seeks out individuals who are committed to joining or forming Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) to help them develop agricultural enterprises.", False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
