2026-06-05 17:20:08.085 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x1654e8b80>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x1320a3b60>>, "[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING CURRICULUM- Zamora.docx - TRAINING METHODOLOGY]\nThe LEAD Project uses a comprehensive training methodology that focuses on practical, hands-on learning tailored to the local context and the needs of its participants.\nCompetency-Based Training (CBET): The training is built around developing specific, measurable skills. It's aligned with the national standards set by the Uganda Vocational Qualifications Framework (UVQF), TVET, and UVITAB. Progress is assessed through direct observation and practical demonstrations, not through traditional exams.\nLearning by Doing: The approach is heavily practical, with 70% of the training taking place in demonstration gardens. The remaining 30% consists of theoretical sessions that use engaging methods like pictorial charts, storytelling, and experience sharing.\nVisual and Participatory Tools: The program uses a variety of interactive tools to make learning accessible and effective. These include role-playing, peer-to-peer teaching, pictorial manuals, demonstration videos, and exchange visits to other farms or projects.\nSeasonal Synchronization: The training schedule is specifically synchronized with Palabek's rainfall and planting seasons. This ensures that participants learn and apply skills—from planting to harvesting—at the most appropriate times, making the training immediately relevant and impactful.\nContinuous Mentorship: To support the replication of skills, trainers conduct weekly visits to participants' household gardens. This ongoing mentorship helps individuals apply what they've learned and troubleshoot any challenges they encounter.\nVSLA Integration: Financial literacy training is introduced midway through the program, and participants are supported in forming Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) within the first six months. This ensures that financial management and group savings are integrated with the agricultural skills they are developing.", False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 17:20:08.088 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #10 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x1654e8b80>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x1320a3b60>>, "[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING CURRICULUM- Zamora.docx - TRAINING METHODOLOGY]\nThe LEAD Project uses a comprehensive training methodology that focuses on practical, hands-on learning tailored to the local context and the needs of its participants.\nCompetency-Based Training (CBET): The training is built around developing specific, measurable skills. It's aligned with the national standards set by the Uganda Vocational Qualifications Framework (UVQF), TVET, and UVITAB. Progress is assessed through direct observation and practical demonstrations, not through traditional exams.\nLearning by Doing: The approach is heavily practical, with 70% of the training taking place in demonstration gardens. The remaining 30% consists of theoretical sessions that use engaging methods like pictorial charts, storytelling, and experience sharing.\nVisual and Participatory Tools: The program uses a variety of interactive tools to make learning accessible and effective. These include role-playing, peer-to-peer teaching, pictorial manuals, demonstration videos, and exchange visits to other farms or projects.\nSeasonal Synchronization: The training schedule is specifically synchronized with Palabek's rainfall and planting seasons. This ensures that participants learn and apply skills—from planting to harvesting—at the most appropriate times, making the training immediately relevant and impactful.\nContinuous Mentorship: To support the replication of skills, trainers conduct weekly visits to participants' household gardens. This ongoing mentorship helps individuals apply what they've learned and troubleshoot any challenges they encounter.\nVSLA Integration: Financial literacy training is introduced midway through the program, and participants are supported in forming Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs) within the first six months. This ensures that financial management and group savings are integrated with the agricultural skills they are developing.", False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
