2026-06-05 17:20:26.828 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | Submitting callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x31ec31cc0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x1320a3b60>>, "[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING CURRICULUM- Zamora.docx - DETAILED SIX-MONTH TRAINING PLAN]\nMONTH 1: ORIENTATION, LAND PREPARATION & FARM PLANNING\nContext: \nPalabek is in a region challenged by a tropical climate and limited fertile land. As both refugee and host communities depend on small-scale farming, the training introduces Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA) to improve food security, nutrition, and income, and build resilience.\nThe LEAD Project will use community learning gardens as a practical tool to introduce these CSA practices with an emphasis on sustainable land use and preparing for market-oriented production.\nObjectives:\nThe first month of the training has two main objectives:\nIntroduce Foundational Concepts: Participants will gain a solid understanding of the project's overall goals and key principles of Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA). This includes learning about essential techniques like soil conservation, water management, and planting resilient crops.\nDevelop Practical Farming Plans: Participants will be guided in creating their own seasonal crop calendars. They'll also select a variety of drought-tolerant crops to plant, such as sorghum, millet, and cassava. Finally, they will work together to design a 1-acre model farm plot for both the host and refugee communities. These plots will serve as hands-on learning hubs and models for participants to replicate in their own household gardens.\nKey Topics\nProject Overview: Participants will learn about the purpose and benefits of the project, including their roles as both refugees and hosts. The discussion will also cover key outputs, such as increased crop yields.\nClimate and Soil Analysis: The training will explore local rainfall and temperature patterns, common soil types, and fertility issues. Participants will learn why soil testing is crucial for choosing the right crops.\nDrought-Tolerant Crops: The curriculum will focus on crops that can withstand dry conditions, such as sweet potatoes and legumes. The discussion will cover their nutritional value, market opportunities (including cross-border trade with South Sudan), and their contribution to both food security and income.\nSustainable Farming Practices: Participants will learn about effective farming methods, comparing organic techniques like composting and mulching with the controlled use of artificial fertilizers. The focus will be on cost-effectiveness and long-term soil health.\nLand Preparation and Design: The training will cover land clearing methods that prevent soil erosion, simple ways to test soil pH and texture, and how to design ridges and furrows to conserve water and promote healthy root growth.\nCropping Patterns: Participants will be introduced to advanced planting strategies like mixed cropping, crop rotation, and agroforestry. These techniques are crucial for improving soil fertility, reducing pests, and maximizing yields.", False) with args () and kwargs {} to thread worker queue
2026-06-05 17:20:26.832 | DEBUG  | logging:callHandlers:1737 | #1 got callback functools.partial(<built-in method run of _contextvars.Context object at 0x31ec31cc0>, <bound method Embedder._embed_sync of <src.embedder.Embedder object at 0x1320a3b60>>, "[SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE TRAINING CURRICULUM- Zamora.docx - DETAILED SIX-MONTH TRAINING PLAN]\nMONTH 1: ORIENTATION, LAND PREPARATION & FARM PLANNING\nContext: \nPalabek is in a region challenged by a tropical climate and limited fertile land. As both refugee and host communities depend on small-scale farming, the training introduces Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA) to improve food security, nutrition, and income, and build resilience.\nThe LEAD Project will use community learning gardens as a practical tool to introduce these CSA practices with an emphasis on sustainable land use and preparing for market-oriented production.\nObjectives:\nThe first month of the training has two main objectives:\nIntroduce Foundational Concepts: Participants will gain a solid understanding of the project's overall goals and key principles of Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA). This includes learning about essential techniques like soil conservation, water management, and planting resilient crops.\nDevelop Practical Farming Plans: Participants will be guided in creating their own seasonal crop calendars. They'll also select a variety of drought-tolerant crops to plant, such as sorghum, millet, and cassava. Finally, they will work together to design a 1-acre model farm plot for both the host and refugee communities. These plots will serve as hands-on learning hubs and models for participants to replicate in their own household gardens.\nKey Topics\nProject Overview: Participants will learn about the purpose and benefits of the project, including their roles as both refugees and hosts. The discussion will also cover key outputs, such as increased crop yields.\nClimate and Soil Analysis: The training will explore local rainfall and temperature patterns, common soil types, and fertility issues. Participants will learn why soil testing is crucial for choosing the right crops.\nDrought-Tolerant Crops: The curriculum will focus on crops that can withstand dry conditions, such as sweet potatoes and legumes. The discussion will cover their nutritional value, market opportunities (including cross-border trade with South Sudan), and their contribution to both food security and income.\nSustainable Farming Practices: Participants will learn about effective farming methods, comparing organic techniques like composting and mulching with the controlled use of artificial fertilizers. The focus will be on cost-effectiveness and long-term soil health.\nLand Preparation and Design: The training will cover land clearing methods that prevent soil erosion, simple ways to test soil pH and texture, and how to design ridges and furrows to conserve water and promote healthy root growth.\nCropping Patterns: Participants will be introduced to advanced planting strategies like mixed cropping, crop rotation, and agroforestry. These techniques are crucial for improving soil fertility, reducing pests, and maximizing yields.", False) with args () and kwargs {} from queue
